/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5570a31d-9d77-518e-a3d9-cd84392d53e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244716} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f82d001-87e2-5d8c-95d5-f927cddbf39c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244712}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5c186d3-f92b-5458-992f-23011e297e18" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-86824" :question/problem "Mike has a box containing $5$ parts of a toy car. In how many ways can Mike select a set of $3$ different parts from the box?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $5$ parts in the box, so $n=5.$ We want to select $3$ of those parts, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential sets of $3$ parts is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4\\cdot \\cancel{3!}}{2!\\cdot \\cancel{3!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
